================================================================================
G4+ - Unified Theorem Prover for Minimal, Intuitionistic and Classical Logic
================================================================================

Version:  1.0
Date:     17 January 2026
Author:   Joseph Vidal-Rosset
Email:    joseph@vidal-rosset.net
Web:      https://www.vidal-rosset.net
License:  GNU General Public License

================================================================================
DESCRIPTION
================================================================================

G4+ is a unified automated theorem prover for minimal, intuitionistic, and
classical first-order logic. It combines the G4 sequent calculus approach
with the nanoCoP connection method to provide:

- Automatic logic level detection (minimal -> intuitionistic -> classical)
- Multiple proof output formats (G4 sequent calculus, natural deduction)
- Support for equality and first-order logic with functions
- Full TPTP format support with include directive processing
- Compatible with SystemOnTPTP

The prover implements:
- G4-mic: A sequent calculus prover for the three logic levels
- nanoCoP 2.0: Jens Otten's connection prover for first-order logic
- Automatic delegation: equality/functions -> nanoCoP, pure logic -> G4-mic

================================================================================
REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================

- SWI-Prolog (version 8.0 or later)
- Unix-like operating system (Linux, macOS, BSD)
- Standard Unix utilities (sh, grep, ps, expr)

================================================================================
INSTALLATION
================================================================================

1. Extract the package:
   tar -xzf g4plus-1.0.tar.gz
   cd g4plus-1.0

2. Make the script executable:
   chmod +x g4plus.sh

3. (Optional) Set the TPTP environment variable for include directives:
   export TPTP=/path/to/TPTP

4. Test this example: 
   ./g4plus.sh test/example_equality.p 

================================================================================
USAGE
================================================================================

Basic usage:
   ./g4plus.sh <problem_file> [<time_limit>]

Parameters:
   problem_file  - TPTP format problem file (.p extension)
   time_limit    - CPU time limit in seconds (default: 100)

Examples:
   ./g4plus.sh problem.p          # Use default 100 second limit
   ./g4plus.sh problem.p 300      # Use 300 second limit
   ./g4plus.sh ~/TPTP/Problems/SET/SET001-1.p 60

================================================================================
TPTP FORMAT SUPPORT
================================================================================

G4+ supports the TPTP language including:

1. First-order form (fof):
   - All standard logical connectives: ~, &, |, =>, <=>
   - Universal quantifier: ![X]: or !X:
   - Existential quantifier: ?[X]: or ?X:
   - Equality: X = Y
   - Functions and predicates

2. Clause normal form (cnf):
   - Standard clause notation

3. Include directives:
   - include('filename.ax')
   - include('filename.ax', [axiom_name1, axiom_name2])

Include file resolution (per TPTP specification):
   - Relative paths are searched:
     a) In the directory of the current file
     b) In the TPTP library root
   - Absolute paths are used as-is

   The TPTP library root is, in order:
     a) a directory set for the session with set_tptp_root/1, e.g.
          ?- set_tptp_root('/path/to/TPTP').
        put this in ~/.config/swi-prolog/init.pl to make it permanent
     b) the $TPTP environment variable

   No default path is compiled into G4+. If an include cannot be
   resolved, G4+ reports every directory it tried, so a missing axiom
   file is never mistaken for an unprovable problem.

Example TPTP problem:
   %------------------------------------------------------------
   % File: example.p
   %------------------------------------------------------------
   include('Axioms/SET001-0.ax').
   
   fof(subset_defn, axiom,
       ![X,Y]: (subset(X,Y) <=> ![Z]: (member(Z,X) => member(Z,Y)))).
   
   fof(prove_transitivity, conjecture,
       ![A,B,C]: ((subset(A,B) & subset(B,C)) => subset(A,C))).

================================================================================
OUTPUT FORMAT
================================================================================

G4+ produces SZS ontology compatible output:

Success cases:
   % SZS status Theorem           - Conjecture proved (FOF)
   % SZS status Unsatisfiable     - Negated formula proved (CNF)

Failure cases:
   % SZS status CounterSatisfiable - Conjecture disproved (FOF)
   % SZS status Satisfiable        - Formula satisfiable (CNF)

Other cases:
   % SZS status Timeout           - Time limit exceeded
   % SZS status Unknown           - Could not determine status
   % SZS status Error             - Parse or system error

Exit codes:
   0 - Theorem/Unsatisfiable (success)
   1 - CounterSatisfiable/Satisfiable (refuted)
   2 - Timeout/Unknown/Error

================================================================================
SystemOnTPTP INTEGRATION
================================================================================

G4+ is designed to work with SystemOnTPTP. The interface is compatible
with the standard SystemOnTPTP calling conventions:

- Input: TPTP format problem files
- Output: SZS ontology status
- Include directives: Fully supported with $TPTP environment variable
- Time limits: CPU time tracking with automatic termination

For SystemOnTPTP administrators:
- Place all .pl files and g4plus.sh in the same directory
- Ensure SWI-Prolog is in the system PATH
- Set appropriate permissions on g4plus.sh
- Configure $TPTP environment variable if using TPTP library

================================================================================
FILES IN DISTRIBUTION
================================================================================

The prover, as delivered -- five files, one entry point:

g4mic_nanocop.pl              - G4-mic core prover (load this one)
i_operators.pl                - operator declarations
nanocop20_swi_for_g4plus.pl   - nanoCoP 2.0 core, adapted for G4+
nanocop_proof.pl              - proof presentation
nanocop_tptp2.pl              - TPTP parser and equality axioms

Also in the repository:

test_suite.pl                 - the 116-test suite; run_tests/0
prover_loader.pl              - loads the prover and the test suite
o_driver.pl and i_*..x_*.pl   - the same prover, split into modules,
                                clause-for-clause identical to
                                g4mic_nanocop.pl; use it to work on the
                                code, not to ship
nanocop_main.pl               - nanoCoP's own entry point, for running
                                nanoCoP alone on a file
nc2tab.pl                     - Beth tableaux from a nanoCoP proof
                                (standalone: beth/1, beth_tree/1)
tinker.{html,js,css,pl}       - the browser front end (SWI-Prolog WASM),
                                with highlight.pl and term.{js,css}
README                        - this file
CLAUDE.md                     - development notes: validation
                                procedure, optimisation history,
                                deployment rules

================================================================================
TECHNICAL DETAILS
================================================================================

Logic Levels:
   1. Minimal Logic: No ex falso quodlibet (_|_ -> A)
   2. Intuitionistic Logic: Minimal + ex falso quodlibet
   3. Classical Logic: Intuitionistic + law of excluded middle

Proof Strategy:
   1. Detect if formula contains equality or complex functions
   2. If yes -> delegate to nanoCoP (connection prover)
   3. If no  -> use G4-mic (sequent calculus)
   4. Try to prove at each logic level: minimal -> intuitionistic -> classical

Time Management:
   - Shell script tracks CPU time using ps command
   - Prolog uses call_with_time_limit for internal timeout
   - Process automatically terminates on time limit

Memory Limits:
   - SWI-Prolog configured with: -L120M -G120M -T100M
   - Limits: 120MB local stack, 120MB global stack, 100MB trail

================================================================================
EXAMPLES
================================================================================

Example 1: Simple propositional logic
   Problem: p & q => q & p
   Command: echo "fof(test,conjecture,(p & q) => (q & p))." > test.p
            ./g4plus.sh test.p
   Output:  % SZS status Theorem

Example 2: First-order logic with quantifiers
   Problem: ![X]:p(X) => ?[Y]:p(Y)
   Command: echo "fof(test,conjecture,![X]:p(X) => ?[Y]:p(Y))." > test.p
            ./g4plus.sh test.p
   Output:  % SZS status Theorem

Example 3: Problem with include directive
   File structure:
      axioms/group.ax     - Group theory axioms
      problem.p           - Problem file with include

   problem.p contains:
      include('axioms/group.ax').
      fof(test, conjecture, ...).

   Command: ./g4plus.sh problem.p 300

================================================================================
TROUBLESHOOTING
================================================================================

Problem: "File not found" error
Solution: - Check file path is correct
          - For include files, ensure $TPTP is set correctly
          - Use absolute paths if needed

Problem: "Timeout" status
Solution: - Increase time limit
          - Problem may be too complex
          - Check if problem is actually valid

Problem: "Parse error"
Solution: - Verify TPTP syntax is correct
          - Check all quantified variables are bound
          - Ensure operators are properly parenthesized

Problem: Script permission denied
Solution: chmod +x g4plus.sh

Problem: SWI-Prolog not found
Solution: - Install SWI-Prolog
          - Ensure 'swipl' is in your PATH
          - Or modify PROLOG_PATH in g4plus.sh

